In modern Angular, Signals introduce fine-grained reactivity. A writable signal holds a value and notifies consumers when updated. `computed()` creates a derived signal that is memoized—it only re-evaluates when a dependency changes and its value is actually read (pull-based evaluation).

`effect()` is for side-effects like syncing with local storage or rendering charts when signal values change.

Traditional Angular relies on Zone.js to monkey-patch all browser async APIs and trigger change detection top-down from the root. With Signals and Zoneless mode (`provideExperimentalZonelessChangeDetection()`), Angular tracks exactly which DOM nodes depend on which signals. When a signal emits, Angular marks only that specific view dirty, eliminating Zone.js runtime overhead.

