The architectural shift between legacy and modern build tools comes down to bundling strategy during development:
1. **Legacy Webpack / Angular CLI (Webpack)**:
   - *Mechanism*: Webpack crawls every file in your application, transpiles TypeScript, bundles everything into in-memory chunks, and only then starts the dev server. As an app grows to 10,000 files, cold dev server starts take 60-120 seconds, and HMR takes seconds per save.
2. **Vite & Modern Angular CLI (Vite + esbuild)**:
   - *Dev Server (Unbundled Native ESM)*: The dev server starts instantly (< 300ms). When the browser loads the page, it requests files natively (`import ./app.js`). Vite intercepts the HTTP request, compiles that single TypeScript file on the fly using esbuild, and serves it.
   - *Dependency Pre-bundling*: Vite uses esbuild (written in Go) to pre-bundle CommonJS/node_modules dependencies into optimized ESM once and caches them with HTTP 304 headers.
   - *Production*: Uses Rollup / esbuild to generate tree-shaken, code-split static assets for production deployment.

