In ASP.NET Core, high throughput depends on not tying up threads during waiting periods. When we call `await _db.Orders.ToListAsync(cancellationToken)`, we are performing non-blocking I/O. The OS kernel and socket handles wait for the database, while our .NET thread is immediately returned to the Thread Pool to process other HTTP requests. When the database responds, an I/O completion port triggers a Thread Pool thread to resume execution at the await point. If developers make the mistake of calling `.Result` or `.Wait()` on an async Task, they synchronously block the thread. Under high traffic, this exhausts all available Thread Pool worker threads—a fatal scenario known as **Thread Pool Starvation**.

### Code Example
